    "text": "If the resistivity of pure silicon is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3000</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>&nbsp;and the mobilities of electrons and holes are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>045</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> respectively, then what will be the resistivity of a specimen of silicon when&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>19</mn></msup></math> atoms of phosphorous are added per cubic metre?",
